 ],
 "literal": "Like the water-moss gathered at the drinking-ghat of the common well drunk by the town, / is the pallor. My lover, / whenever and wherever he touches, it recedes, / and whenever and wherever he leaves, because of its spreading.",
 "translation": "My pallor is just like the water-moss that gathers at the drinking-ghat of the common well drunk by the town: whenever my lover touches me, it recedes; the moment he leaves, it spreads all over.",
 "modern": "ஊர் மக்கள் குடிக்கும் கிணற்றின் நீர் உண்ணும் துறையில் குவிந்திருக்கும் பாசியைப் போன்றது என் உடலின் பசலை நிறம். காதலர் தொடும் போதெல்லாம் அது விலகிப் போகிறது; அவர் கையை எடுக்கும் போதெல்லாம் அது மீண்டும் பரவி விடுகிறது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to her companion about the agonizing nature of her love-sickness. She compares her spreading pallor to the water-moss in a busy public well. Just as the moss parts when a vessel is dipped to draw water and immediately closes back when the vessel is removed, her pallor disappears the moment her lover touches her, only to return instantly when he lets go. This vivid image from the agricultural landscape perfectly captures the fleeting relief of union and the immediate return of the pain of separation. The key words are 'pāci' (moss), 'pasalai' (pallor), 'toṭuvuḻi' (whenever touched), and 'viṭuvuḻi' (whenever left).",
